      "id": "bbc-2026-06-27-amber-extreme-heat-warning-extended-before-cooldown-ends-rec",
      "title": "Amber extreme heat warning extended before cooldown ends record-breaking heatwave",
      "summary": "The record-breaking heatwave is set to end with cooler and more unsettled weather by \nSunday.",
      "body": "Amber extreme heat warning extended before cooldown ends record-breaking heatwaveImage source, Getty ImagesByElizabeth RizziniLead Weather PresenterAn amber extreme heat warning issued by the Met Office has been extended as scorching temperatures cling on in parts of the UK.But cooler air is set to arrive by the second half of the weekend, ending the spell of record-breaking heat.It will take its time to reach eastern and south-eastern England where it will remain searingly hot throughout Saturday. A Met Office amber warning for extreme heat in effect across south-east England and East Anglia has been extended until 09:00 BST on Sunday. It comes as another hot and humid night is expected for the region on Saturday.  Cooler for many but not all on Saturday Temperatures on Saturday morning were in the mid to high teens for many, although still around 20C for eastern England and there were some overnight thunderstorms too.High pressure, which has been responsible for the sunshine and heat, is starting to retreat eastwards and low pressure is centred to the north-west of the UK, bringing weather fronts in from the Atlantic. The wind will be turning from a southerly to more of a westerly bringing about the change to cooler conditions.Image source, BBC WeatherImage caption, It will remain hot in the east on Saturday, albeit cooler than earlier on in the week.Despite a cloudy start, Scotland will see a mixture of sunny spells and scattered showers developing for most, heavy in places and locally thundery.Northern Ireland will also see outbreaks of showery rain and some sunny spells.There will be a moderate to fresh south-westerly wind in the north and west of the UK and across Scotland, Northern Ireland, western Wales and the far north and south-west of England will tend to peak in the low twenties.Central, eastern and south-east England will still be hot with afternoon temperatures in the high twenties or low thirties. Here the winds will be lighter and  there will be more strong sunshine with the chance of some isolated thunderstorms.When will the heatwave end?Sunday marks the end of the current heatwave for all of us. Weather fronts will move eastwards overnight into Sunday on south-westerly winds bringing cloud and scattered showers across the UK. Some of the showers could be heavy especially across southern England. There will be some sunshine ahead of and behind the front which will drag behind it the cooler air. Scotland and Northern Ireland will see the mid to high teens, with possibly the low twenties in eastern Scotland.  Image source, BBC WeatherImage caption, Sunday's temperatures will be closer to the seasonal average but still warm in the eastEngland and Wales will be warmer with temperatures in the low twenties for many. There will be relief at last from the extreme heat in the east and south-east of England with temperatures in the mid-twenties. 27C is still possible in East Anglia.Check the full forecast where you live here.Will there be more heatwaves this summer?There have been two UK heatwaves already this year resulting in new national monthly records. There is still a whole lot more summer to go too, with meteorological autumn not beginning until 1 September.Long-term forecasts for the next two months suggest that significant bursts of heat are likely and that temperatures will be above average.According to the Met Office, a hotter summer is twice as likely as at the beginning of the century.More on this story",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-06-27T10:38:36.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_weather/1200/cpsprodpb/ab7f/live/10f0a900-7191-11f1-8268-a773f3902180.png",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/weather/articles/crkvl343jj3o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
