      "id": "hackernews-2026-06-24-blogging-can-just-be-stating-the-obvious",
      "title": "Blogging can just be stating the obvious",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://blog.jim-nielsen.com/2026/blogging-stating-the-obvious/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=48666927\nPoints: 281\n# Comments: 97",
      "body": "John Gruber writes about those annoying popups every website seems to have now and while he does a great job tearing into these ubiquitous, user-hostile patterns, one of the things that stood out to me about his piece was this meta commentary on blogging. Here’s John:\nIt’s funny how often blogging feels like being the little child in the story of The Emperor’s New Clothes. You’re just stating what seems obvious to you.\nI often look at my own posts and think, “There’s nothing novel, or important, or deep in here at all — is this even worth saying?”\nA post’s point can seem so glaringly obvious to me (and thus, I presume, others) it feels like a waste of time to even say it. As John says:\nA webpage should show the webpage. An email should show the email. I should not have to explain this.\nBut then real-world examples of annoyance pile up around you and nobody talks about it, so you finally just have to say it in a post and bring receipts.\nYou feel like someone gone mad: “Is anyone else seeing the same thing I’m seeing? And we’re just ok with this?”\nVery often, those are the best posts I read from others.\nSo it must be that a key ingredient to blogging is simple: have a willingness to state something that seems obvious to you but nobody else is saying it.\nOr if someone else is saying it, just link to them and say, “Yes!!! This!!!”",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Curiositry",
      "publishedAt": "2026-06-24T23:46:37.000Z",
      "imageUrl": null,
      "originalUrl": "https://blog.jim-nielsen.com/2026/blogging-stating-the-obvious/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
